From d4626d61bf8cbb91482627f88aa8f8e0fb18a416 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:34:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Start a toplevel's window geometry at its own surface's
 origin
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hyprland places a toplevel by its surface's origin and ignores the
window geometry's offset. The content was the toplevel's surface, with
the menu bar (and the header, where Sidestep draws one) on subsurfaces
above it and the geometry starting at their top, a negative y: there the
bar was drawn above the place the window was given, under the desktop's
own bar, and as much was left empty at the bottom.

A toplevel's own surface is now a frame: exactly the window geometry,
one transparent pixel stretched over it, taking no pointer input. The
content's surface sits on it under the title bar, and the scroll tiles,
the decorations, the menu bar and sheets are subsurfaces of the frame
too, side by side: Hyprland draws a tree of subsurfaces a level at a
time, so tiles nested under the content would cover sheets. The
geometry starts at (0, 0) everywhere. Everything but sheets is
synchronized with the frame, which commits last, so a resize still shows
the content, its tiles and decorations, the frame's size and the
content's place in it together; when the title bar changes the content,
tiles, sheets and input-method carets move with it.

Under the headless sway, which honours the geometry's offset, the
menus-panels menubar, alert-sheet and context scenarios, tiled and
floating with client-side decorations, at scales 1, 1.5 and 2, are
pixel-identical before and after, and wayland_system passes; the
protocol trace shows set_window_geometry(0, -26, …) before and
(0, 0, …) after.

Signed-off-by: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
